1
00:00:01,062 --> 00:00:06,387
Hello everyone, I'm Sérgio Fernandes, and
this is our project, IRIS Production

2
00:00:06,387 --> 00:00:11,848
Guardian. It is an application built
entirely on the InterSystems IRIS platform.

3
00:00:11,848 --> 00:00:17,445
It aims to solve a real problem: when an
integration environment fails and someone

4
00:00:17,445 --> 00:00:23,247
needs to understand what happened as quickly
as possible. You can't rely on a generic

5
00:00:23,247 --> 00:00:28,572
answer. Our application is made up of three
modules. The first is the Monitor,

6
00:00:28,646 --> 00:00:36,202
which is real-time monitoring of the state
of each Production. The second is the AI

7
00:00:36,202 --> 00:00:43,758
Incident Investigator: when a component
fails, we select that component, and within

8
00:00:43,758 --> 00:00:51,678
a time window it analyzes real failure data
and shows a structured investigation, where

9
00:00:51,678 --> 00:00:56,412
you can see the most likely hypothesis for
the cause

10
00:00:56,486 --> 00:01:03,918
and what cannot be stated with certainty.
But it never fixes anything on its own; it

11
00:01:03,918 --> 00:01:11,527
always depends on a human action. The third
is the RAG Assistant. It answers questions

12
00:01:11,527 --> 00:01:19,312
about the project's own documentation. So we
can ask this Assistant any kind of question

13
00:01:19,312 --> 00:01:25,948
and it can only answer within the scope of
the project, nothing outside it.

14
00:01:26,022 --> 00:01:34,130
We even created two versions of the query,
one with Gemini and another with Groq. Shall

15
00:01:34,130 --> 00:01:42,144
we go to the application? Here we are with
our Production, and what we are going to do

16
00:01:42,144 --> 00:01:49,972
is simulate an interoperability transfer, an
integration transfer. The file comes in

17
00:01:49,972 --> 00:01:51,836
through the service,

18
00:01:51,910 --> 00:01:55,628
it is processed here in

19
00:01:56,166 --> 00:02:03,864
this processing host, and then it goes to
the operation. Here we have a rule: if the

20
00:02:03,864 --> 00:02:11,745
message has low priority, it comes here to
the File Output, but if it has high, medium

21
00:02:11,745 --> 00:02:19,626
or critical priority, then it goes here to
the priority output. So this is the rule we

22
00:02:19,626 --> 00:02:21,276
created inside the

23
00:02:21,350 --> 00:02:29,098
IRIS interoperability application itself. So
here we have the severity: high, critical

24
00:02:29,098 --> 00:02:36,396
and medium. Those end up here in the
Priority Output. And anything different from

25
00:02:36,396 --> 00:02:43,694
that ends up in the Output. So the first
thing we are going to do is bring up our

26
00:02:43,694 --> 00:02:45,676
application, start it.

27
00:02:46,630 --> 00:02:54,141
And here we have our Production active,
refreshing every 10 seconds. So the

28
00:02:54,141 --> 00:03:02,153
Production is active. Next step: we are
going to create traffic every 10 seconds

29
00:03:02,153 --> 00:03:10,566
with normal priority. So at this moment
there are files here in the File Output, and

30
00:03:10,566 --> 00:03:11,868
we can follow

31
00:03:11,942 --> 00:03:16,588
how the output folders are doing.

32
00:03:17,094 --> 00:03:19,020
So, first of all...

33
00:03:22,726 --> 00:03:28,940
the output folder without priority already
has two files, now three,

34
00:03:30,246 --> 00:03:33,260
and the priority one is empty.

35
00:03:35,494 --> 00:03:38,220
What we are going to do now is

36
00:03:39,366 --> 00:03:45,292
send a high-criticality message.

37
00:03:49,254 --> 00:03:54,060
So when we send it there with the critical
type,

38
00:03:54,726 --> 00:03:55,820
it goes

39
00:03:57,254 --> 00:04:01,324
in our application to the Priority.

40
00:04:02,150 --> 00:04:05,452
So if we take a look at the folder now,

41
00:04:06,022 --> 00:04:10,380
there is a file in there, which is our
INC-003.

42
00:04:14,022 --> 00:04:21,366
Now the next step is to simulate the error,
so what we are going to do is remove the

43
00:04:21,366 --> 00:04:25,388
folder permission, simulating a server
outage.

44
00:04:28,454 --> 00:04:30,796
So we take the folder down.

45
00:04:31,270 --> 00:04:35,372
and try to send a message to it again.

46
00:04:40,806 --> 00:04:49,930
Note that our processing host and also our
Priority Output will become degraded. Let me

47
00:04:49,930 --> 00:04:52,972
just change the message name.

48
00:04:53,670 --> 00:04:57,516
because that message already exists in 03/0

49
00:05:10,822 --> 00:05:18,741
The messages keep piling up, because it has
now detected that this destination is not

50
00:05:18,741 --> 00:05:26,660
up, it went down, and this is where the AI
investigation effectively begins. So if we

51
00:05:26,660 --> 00:05:29,548
now come to the Investigator...

52
00:05:31,366 --> 00:05:38,092
We ask it to run an analysis, for example,
of the last five minutes of our operation.

53
00:05:39,270 --> 00:05:47,796
So it will collect the evidence, create an
analysis with the AI and bring us the

54
00:05:47,796 --> 00:05:56,855
problems. It reported a timeout exceeded
failure, which is exactly the write attempt.

55
00:05:56,855 --> 00:06:04,316
And here it gives the possible errors. So
there is the summary section

56
00:06:04,806 --> 00:06:12,728
where it explains what the component's
problem is. It said there was a failure

57
00:06:12,728 --> 00:06:21,563
longer than 15 seconds to write the INC-004
file that we placed in the priority folder.

58
00:06:21,563 --> 00:06:30,297
In the observations, it tells us what the
current health is and what the event was, so

59
00:06:30,297 --> 00:06:31,516
this one had

60
00:06:31,654 --> 00:06:40,872
an error, this other one was a success, so
it has all of its observations regarding the

61
00:06:40,872 --> 00:06:49,772
latest events of the last five minutes,
okay? So it reported here "cannot open file"

62
00:06:53,958 --> 00:06:56,204
And then it tells us where

63
00:06:56,806 --> 00:06:59,532
the error is loaded.

64
00:07:01,478 --> 00:07:10,151
What are the hypotheses for this error?
First, it says here that it cannot write,

65
00:07:10,151 --> 00:07:18,931
the destination is not accessible, this
Priority Output. So it understands that it

66
00:07:18,931 --> 00:07:26,748
may be, for example, that it is just read-
only. Its second hypothesis...

67
00:07:26,822 --> 00:07:29,260
and

68
00:07:31,750 --> 00:07:34,924
unprocessed messages.

69
00:07:35,974 --> 00:07:44,708
So it is putting forward a hypothesis that
it could not write because of unprocessed

70
00:07:44,708 --> 00:07:45,644
messages.

71
00:07:48,518 --> 00:07:50,988
The third one is...

72
00:07:51,686 --> 00:08:00,566
that the path is bad, right? So the path is
not correct. For example, if we switched the

73
00:08:00,566 --> 00:08:09,546
path to the Alt, it knows the Alt is up. The
Alt Priority is not. It knows that. So if we

74
00:08:09,546 --> 00:08:14,188
switched to this path here, it could solve
it.

75
00:08:15,206 --> 00:08:21,740
And also, automatic recovery cannot be
triggered.

76
00:08:23,014 --> 00:08:31,803
So it says here that the permissions must be
fixed so that the incidents are resolved.

77
00:08:31,803 --> 00:08:40,695
What can it not state? The gaps section. It
needs to confirm the hypotheses. So it asks

78
00:08:40,695 --> 00:08:49,382
us to check the components, the component
states. It also asks to check the priority,

79
00:08:49,382 --> 00:08:51,324
the priority paths.

80
00:08:51,398 --> 00:09:00,573
It also asks us to look at the non-priority
outputs, since it could be an isolated

81
00:09:00,573 --> 00:09:10,195
problem with the priority path. There are
manual interventions and configurations that

82
00:09:10,195 --> 00:09:17,020
it asks us to review. In fact, it could not
check all of this

83
00:09:17,094 --> 00:09:24,529
because of a permission failure, or whatever
the reason, and so it lists this under gaps,

84
00:09:24,529 --> 00:09:31,714
what it did not verify. And here it comes
with the suggested next steps, what to do to

85
00:09:31,714 --> 00:09:38,647
solve it. The first one, in fact, is already
the error we simulated, we removed the

86
00:09:38,647 --> 00:09:42,908
folder permission, but it also gives other
details.

87
00:09:42,982 --> 00:09:51,316
Check logs, check pending messages, it even
created a query for us. So notice that the

88
00:09:51,316 --> 00:09:59,553
AI brings us suggestions of what to do; it
does not do it automatically, but the gain

89
00:09:59,553 --> 00:10:07,209
for someone in support, for example, is
enormous, because you have a ready-made

90
00:10:07,209 --> 00:10:09,244
analysis in real time

91
00:10:09,318 --> 00:10:17,542
of your running operation, and you can
simply follow the step-by-step to solve the

92
00:10:17,542 --> 00:10:25,465
problem concretely, okay? There is also a
documentation section showing what it

93
00:10:25,465 --> 00:10:33,991
consulted. This is part of the software we
built. And now we go to the RAG Assistant,

94
00:10:33,991 --> 00:10:34,492
okay?

95
00:10:34,566 --> 00:10:40,828
So here I will not ask that question,
because that question is exactly the answer

96
00:10:40,828 --> 00:10:47,168
already in the Investigator, but we can do
it here. Let's take a look at the error

97
00:10:47,168 --> 00:10:50,028
number, for example, from the message

98
00:10:51,302 --> 00:10:54,988
let me search here for the message that has
the error, this one,

99
00:10:55,622 --> 00:11:06,380
whether we can display error 5005 here,
okay? So let's ask it what error 5005 is.

100
00:11:07,110 --> 00:11:09,068
"Jewel"

101
00:11:11,526 --> 00:11:13,100
5005

102
00:11:15,334 --> 00:11:20,172
Now our RAG answers us.

103
00:11:33,286 --> 00:11:34,156
okay

104
00:11:37,030 --> 00:11:42,796
It tells us what the error is, it says here
that it is a timeout,

105
00:11:47,910 --> 00:11:50,284
and gives us all the detail

106
00:11:51,334 --> 00:11:56,812
and what it is consulting in our
documentation file. Alright?

107
00:11:57,542 --> 00:12:06,436
Okay? So what we are going to do now is
grant the permission again and send the

108
00:12:06,436 --> 00:12:08,012
message. Okay?

109
00:12:13,862 --> 00:12:16,300
So we set it again.

110
00:12:17,766 --> 00:12:21,676
755 on the Priority Output folder

111
00:12:24,774 --> 00:12:26,860
and send

112
00:12:27,622 --> 00:12:29,740
the message again.

113
00:12:30,310 --> 00:12:34,316
Now we are going to change its number to
number 5.

114
00:12:41,510 --> 00:12:49,050
There it is, we can already see that
communication has been restored. If we

115
00:12:49,050 --> 00:12:51,564
follow the messages here,

116
00:12:52,774 --> 00:12:59,180
it was completed. That error message stays
indicated there,

117
00:12:59,782 --> 00:13:01,484
its error,

118
00:13:06,758 --> 00:13:11,468
which we can even resend. Now the service is
up.

119
00:13:12,134 --> 00:13:14,316
there will be no more problem.

120
00:13:20,486 --> 00:13:28,256
So notice that the first time it hit an
error, 5005, and then recovered, okay? Well

121
00:13:28,256 --> 00:13:35,838
folks, this is our Production Guardian
application. It is made up of the Monitor,

122
00:13:35,838 --> 00:13:43,608
which follows in real time the messages
passing through, with a 10-second delay, so

123
00:13:43,608 --> 00:13:45,948
it has already refreshed,

124
00:13:46,022 --> 00:13:55,918
the Investigator, which brings us the
summary, observations, hypotheses, gaps and

125
00:13:55,918 --> 00:14:06,181
suggested next steps, okay? And two RAGs so
that we can ask questions related to the

126
00:14:06,181 --> 00:14:11,068
project. If we ask another question here

127
00:14:29,286 --> 00:14:37,577
it does not answer, it is outside the
context of our Production. So folks, thank

128
00:14:37,577 --> 00:14:45,868
you for watching our Production Guardian
project, see you next time, a hug, bye!
